[Music]  more money is flowing into new startups  than ever before but why is that and can  all of them make money i'm alex webb and  this is the attention economy where we  look at all the ways that global  business is fighting for your attention  last year venture capitalists poured 1.4  billion dollars into publishing startups  in the us and europe more than double  the amount of any prior year there's  been an explosion of well capitalized  news startups with names like airmail  puck punchbowl protocol grid the  information tortoise and axios not  forget of course sub stack which lets  writers create subscription newsletters  even my boss's boss's boss justin smith  is getting in on the act and leaving to  found his own new startup and  established media companies are paying a  lot of money to acquire some of these  firms the new york times just agreed to  acquire sports website the athletic for  550 million dollars and axel springer  the german giant wrote a check last year  for a billion dollars to acquire  politico the thing is has anything  meaningfully changed in the economics of  news well the short answer is no not  really what has changed slightly is the  approach the one thing these websites  mostly have in common is a keen focus on  a given subject matter so the athletic  as you might guess  focuses on sports in the us and uk the  information well that's a tech website  originating in silicon valley pup  meanwhile describes itself as being at  the nexus of hollywood wall street  silicon valley and washington they tend  to hire established journalists who they  hope will bring an audience with them  politico is the og here what started as  a dc news sheet has since evolved into a  glossy chronicle of politics on both  sides of the atlantic the key is to  build a passionate audience if you can  do that you can maybe circumvent some of  the tech gatekeepers like google  facebook and twitter by convincing  people to come straight to your website  and if you can get that loyal audience  you can then think about how to make  money from them in fact it's a playbook  straight out of silicon valley there are  fundamentally four ways to make money  firstly of course digital subscriptions  the new york times has turned that into  a 600 million dollar a year business  then there's licensing where you can  think about selling your stories to  other publications  then you've got the advertising business  of course not quite as lucrative as it  once was and finally commerce where you  can think about selling some of the  products that your journalists review  that last one is the business model for  future plc it's britain's best  performing stock of the past five years  it's returned 2 000 to investors and  snapped up established magazine brands  like the week horse and hound and  country life in the process the key  seems to be to start small focus on a  given subject and do it well before  thinking about expanding if you can do  that it's lower risk for investors and  in the world of remote work and cloud  computing a small amount of capital can  go a long way  [Music]

